WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Government programs benefit the poor at the&nbsp;
expense of the rich. Those are the terms on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.040
which many public welfare programs are&nbsp;
sold. But is that really the case? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.960
economist Milton Friedman doesn’t think&nbsp;
so and called this the Robin Hood myth.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.920
Friedman argued that instead of taking&nbsp;
from the rich to help the poor, most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.960
welfare programs benefit the middle-class&nbsp;
at the expense of the very poor and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:36.360
very rich. This happens because of a&nbsp;
simple rule, known as Director‘s law.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.460
In a democratic system, laws are passed by 51&nbsp;
percent of the people voting one way against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:50.100
49 percent of the people voting another.&nbsp;
You might think the bottom 51 percent will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.140
pass laws at the expense of the top 49&nbsp;
percent, but that is rarely the case.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.440
Directors law implies that the bottom 51&nbsp;
percent tend to not only be less fortunate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.080
but also less likely to have the ability to&nbsp;
organize themselves and be politically successful.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:15.060
The top 51 percent are able to use their&nbsp;
power to control politics. But they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:20.340
by definition, also most comfortable&nbsp;
with how things are and hence often the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.320
last to seek political change. The most&nbsp;
effective people in political activity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.860
are running from the lower middle&nbsp;
class through the upper-middle class.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:37.620
They are the people who are literate, who write&nbsp;
the news, who envy the very rich, and who seek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:43.680
political change. And what kind of policies&nbsp;
do they want? Welfare programs for themselves.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.940
One famous policy that runs throughout&nbsp;
almost all democratic societies is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.860
state-financed higher education. It is&nbsp;
sold on the grounds of providing equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:01.080
opportunities to everybody to get an&nbsp;
education. But what are the facts? Who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.700
is really benefiting? Who are the people&nbsp;
who are attending public universities?

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.320
It’s usually people who come from the middle,&nbsp;
and upper-class families. Occasionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.400
there is someone from a lower-class&nbsp;
family, but that’s a small fraction

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.300
Children from middle and&nbsp;
upper-income families often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.120
attend public education for the longest periods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.880
gaining new skills and building strong networks&nbsp;
that enable them to get higher-paying jobs.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:38.160
The taxpayers pay for it, including parents&nbsp;
from the bottom of the income distribution whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.860
children are unlikely to attend college and in&nbsp;
the rare instance that they do so, often drop out.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.340
Friedmann called state-financed&nbsp;
higher education a program to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.440
impose taxes on the people in Watts to send&nbsp;
the children from Beverly Hills to college.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.540
What are your thoughts? Was Friedmann right? And&nbsp;
if so, what’s a good alternative to help create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.100
equal opportunities in a democracy?&nbsp;
Share your thoughts in the comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

